From ec230c2e96c42288433bcaeb9b86a204ae6555c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 01:14:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [DVBridge] Send no level 5 block where the RPU has none

Where the RPU of a frame had no level 5 block (active area), the
serializer made one for the raster: the place of the picture, which for
a picture that fills the 3840x2160 raster is a block of zeros. Such a
block describes nothing that the RPU does not (CCM 001 6.2.2 takes the
active area as the whole picture without one), but displays do not
treat the two alike. Measured by fandangos on an LG C1 and a Hisense TV
with a colorimeter and the TVs' own captures, with an RPU whose level 2
trim has a positive lift: without a level 5 block the lift is left
aside, with a whole-frame one it is applied (the shadows 11.8 to 13.1
times brighter); the TVs' own players and an OPPO UDP-203 send none.
Reported against another build in its issue #212; Hades did the same
(dvbridge_dv_made_area()).

dvbridge_dv_made_area() now makes no block where the four margins of the
place are 0: the metadata go out as the RPU has them. It decides on the
place only, not on what is drawn over the bars, so that the block does
not come and go with subtitles or the OSD. Where the player puts bars
around a picture placed with its own shape, the block of those bars is
made as before, and an RPU's own level 5 block (zeros included, and
offsets left aside for a video) is sent as before.

Hades' own metadata (HDR10, HDR10+ and HLG sent as Dolby Vision, the
menus) got the block of the whole picture from the serializer too, and
keep it: dvbridge_profile81_metadata() now writes a level 5 block of
its own (the area given, or zeros), so what those displays are sent is
byte for byte what they were sent before (dvbridge_measured_apply()
fills the same block with the measured area, as it did).

dvbridge_sent_blocks() reads a payload without a level 5 block back with
DVBRIDGE_SENT_NO_L5 in its values; the player information then shows
"L5 sent: none", and kodi.log says so once a stream.

Host tests: a new test of these cases fails 4 of 16 checks against the
bridge before and passes 17 of 17 after, with only the payloads of an
RPU without level 5 on the raster changed; the battery's serializer
test, adapted, passes 111 of 111 (2 of its new checks fail before), and
211 of its 255 frames differ, each by exactly the 13 bytes of the block.

Thanks to fandangos for the finding and the measurements.
